  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1228 AM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nThe front is slowly moving across the James valley and will \neventually traverse the rest of the CWA by daylight. Cold advection \nis rather weak, and BUFKIT profiles indicate there will be an \nextremely shallow inversion to start the day, with temperatures in \nthe 50s. Thus, with favorable mixing on a west/northwest wind, it \nnot being very cold and the inversion will mix out rapidly... we \nshall see temperatures jump quickly through the 60s into the 70s. \nWhile we did overachieve in comparison to the NBM yesterday, this is \na different regime and as such should not be a benchmark on how well \nthe NBM is doing going forwards. Same with dewpoints, though \nupstream we're seeing 30s replace the 50s in place so there is some \ndry advection. Mixed winds only top out around 15 to 20kts, with NBM \nwinds around 15 to 25mph. Could be close to Red Flag up in \nCorson/Dewey so will need to be monitored. \n\nMild for mid week, with light northerly flow Wednesday and a shift \nto southerly for Thursday. Focus shifts to the pattern change coming \nthis weekend/next week as a broad upper low cuts off over the \nwestern CONUS. This initially amplifies the ridge overhead with well \nabove normal (15 to 20F) temperatures Saturday. Deterministic \nGFS/EC/Canadian bring the core of warmest 850mb air, which is just \njust just barely 2 standard deviations above climo, Friday night, \nmeaning some uncertainty on when the hotter air hits. NBM 25th-75th \npercentile range runs some 7 to 10 degrees Friday and 3 to 7 degrees \nSaturday. Still some difference on when frontal passage flips the \nscript as well and temperatures drop to seasonal if not slightly \nbelow normal (Monday NBM forecast 5 to 10 below normal). Sundays \n25th-75th range for highs jumps to 15 to 23 degrees.",
